#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/id #uuid "a285ae3e-4f06-5e09-a06f-b2620be99888" :question/math-academy-id "e-5504" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/28c1e8d1a466398336c357726d7f345a39e6ace73ab4b36147328b3f0a7313b9.png)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/5611ead246c3e5a26d991603a68f3326b1c381d44eddd116316ca7ea6427fdd8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/65491fb3de59e45bffb4774aca31cc25f20f555f18ffafc8d56221c689520380.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/3798d5e0b3305070161e2df70796a20a8fcd1e11f11cecdc94d59db520582ca3.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/1448624dbf0dbb227d5e2a3f082dbda389c521d905b1eee1aafe9a2226e25cfd.png)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "3dd8d6e6-76a2-591c-90a5-86074539686b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5505" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/62f202b2fdbe406e4785cc4acf7f2b9aaa09980bc894340716fbbe5600fb8ae9.png)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/2f943468de2124874c2d806db25c6ca91a2df9a74902af95220e4974e5ef483b.png)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/6f73d40195a91530d761d966db886e9ad2d87d7fc1059a4112a0a2701ce6a72f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124261 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/id #uuid "b3307ae8-eb7d-533a-aef2-c7f50dea07e2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5506" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/7d992806d0d5b90b65030f8c0080f105f67fbb7b4d3d807c3a719307e0c31e40.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/fdf2311a1010ed8e8f25623328171687a63ea5da80f6e18a1a8dfbff9e5fae42.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/34e8934a56885ec189cc1819b1336f5b52b7708a092233e3c20e0960218cad10.png)\n\nFinally, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ec378af1062dda61e89d060d565ef70bf7593a723bd3cba78dfd86f0d027dc5d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124303 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "8d2aff64-bf98-560b-867d-6b47ccc7dbe7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7330" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ca47231d1f55afd5641f3924cae2252af74467ba29adbbdb51832cbd4cf06663.png) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/b88fd696293e4151a71ca12d25cb56821ba034e07a876777fa30d5d6f6b9c4c3.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/38941ad5615365437a1aee93f085b33d6a240c5a61ce8e0f0a10acb465de0f18.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/a3ea884b0f6ba11582eb72e6cffcc871a144e55c446aaa304e1c7f9face13c7b.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/cc87c4f07116bf301d115ffeb00e81f9cd25d21cb40f61fdbc310f86557a61b4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c97909-11ee-5c2a-9d1e-863b1ffc9516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363686} {:answer/id #uuid "774a9572-928c-59be-8851-b28589a4fade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363687} {:answer/id #uuid "a04e9141-6bc8-5ba3-a6b8-4e835b9616e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363688} {:answer/id #uuid "98dec585-2908-558c-8fee-97e66f48adb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363689} {:answer/id #uuid "9623059f-2139-53ad-a5d8-7bdf66cce09b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "774a9572-928c-59be-8851-b28589a4fade" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1997f22-fce2-54e9-8b3c-64e2c77673cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5be1581-2907-53a1-96d1-84786f5058d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140135" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9908dedc-e3c4-5014-a85f-cc5aed89572f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363693} {:answer/id #uuid "323f7789-4192-5666-8644-89b915896130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363694} {:answer/id #uuid "6fb14d8f-2fec-55ab-a724-1cdfe6f2ef7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363695} {:answer/id #uuid "08ae4e5c-227e-5275-bf06-5285f1e8d8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363696} {:answer/id #uuid "8fcde8bd-593c-5b86-8dcf-7f9230291a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08ae4e5c-227e-5275-bf06-5285f1e8d8f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363696} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1985070-6324-54ee-bf3b-7b23efef3c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65bfb5b4-8e98-560e-a200-cd992b31b578" :question/math-academy-id "q-140148" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 9 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & a \\\\ 1 & 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=9,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$ Computing $A-10I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 9 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ \nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2b=-4+2(4)=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3577329c-4aff-5594-b32c-7bf70ae6ca34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363700} {:answer/id #uuid "4f47a5e4-ffdb-53ab-bb47-6683fb558523" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363701} {:answer/id #uuid "8b46387f-ac66-5c72-be05-e17da61e810f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363702} {:answer/id #uuid "26a290d5-25df-5f52-9824-623fb9d2ad79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363703} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f97d95-f0ef-5e63-8469-c25e6f3f792f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a290d5-25df-5f52-9824-623fb9d2ad79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363703} :answer-field/id #uuid "74c1661c-5ca1-53c6-887c-23fb7a81864c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f5c4210-464b-5376-870a-440393a4aae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140261" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97d6e262-29a2-56f4-9f45-19519e1ccdaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122777} {:answer/id #uuid "bb3146c0-67bd-5977-a9e5-b7ac3eb98c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122778} {:answer/id #uuid "cad04495-c13d-5af4-ae64-4510fd7e5a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779} {:answer/id #uuid "f75f6779-aa2a-58cd-9014-d55bed56dae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122780} {:answer/id #uuid "6548c518-7cc8-5566-a9a4-0122eb0d4e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cad04495-c13d-5af4-ae64-4510fd7e5a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122779} :answer-field/id #uuid "81d95111-fa09-5102-8ef4-d9b3d60d291d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122776}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b036f0f-ab37-53c9-a8c1-aa9fe52bf1e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-37250" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9b0536-0884-56b6-8a24-e5bc5ce50a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363707} {:answer/id #uuid "fe54ea33-88ff-5db8-9b67-7c448be76436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363708} {:answer/id #uuid "06e39217-4094-5d3a-8f77-7e2ef7081c2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363709} {:answer/id #uuid "54b556cc-0d8a-5677-a4c4-0f89f2babf64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363710} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3ab69-745b-5752-8bd0-4a810f38b1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e3ab69-745b-5752-8bd0-4a810f38b1e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363711} :answer-field/id #uuid "43642fdb-7d7e-50e2-8768-f428591a44a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "874949a9-baa2-57d2-b0be-9f3951630221" :question/math-academy-id "q-37256" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+18) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial  $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99143319-a5dc-5a1b-a641-88805ae7651f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122813} {:answer/id #uuid "c74d2f8a-ce07-5224-b401-3f2f744516fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122814} {:answer/id #uuid "f93367ea-485b-512b-9a37-9d13ff2a3f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122815} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6a6893-be6f-560b-9b8c-d18b5e7f771d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122816} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8b2e62-048a-5bdc-b7ab-39c551135f9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93367ea-485b-512b-9a37-9d13ff2a3f71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122815} :answer-field/id #uuid "31f18fd6-ee4f-5f68-b7e0-5b41f1315ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2deb6a7-2499-5dc5-960d-304a334f12e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37273" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da4e0b31-3e56-55a1-b233-43ed465d374e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363713} {:answer/id #uuid "37504856-d640-586b-a556-4544e3527300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363714} {:answer/id #uuid "5e410ebc-731b-59ec-9e5b-4cc2324ec7ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363715} {:answer/id #uuid "cf92bd5b-334f-5928-ade5-7a949a88ff07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363716} {:answer/id #uuid "92509958-184a-56da-b4c5-b7ea76a94457" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4e0b31-3e56-55a1-b233-43ed465d374e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363713} :answer-field/id #uuid "273a45ff-9de8-5a69-92a3-78de7b49d576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363712}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a033518a-f4fb-5fd7-9472-bd2a3557ddc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37275" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50fa5f46-7e8b-5a65-8789-6b75b5f99baf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363720} {:answer/id #uuid "350fe377-4cef-5f0c-b869-d171f7b67004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363721} {:answer/id #uuid "0a543dce-9c93-50bf-819f-a410fbd48d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363722} {:answer/id #uuid "7a37c77e-31e9-554e-8d80-005d1b4cb980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363723} {:answer/id #uuid "04d44da6-62f0-5008-a143-ac77f90aa360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a543dce-9c93-50bf-819f-a410fbd48d44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363722} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd142a5-9995-5a44-9329-82fb63673c8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363719}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13072b88-31f9-5595-bc7b-1289a2913b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-37845" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.  \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "250aeb94-26b3-5db7-a268-7dc6ced96fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363727} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9e8c90-eba9-54ef-a6a6-4d6f96b0d94e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363728} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab4520b-2eaf-5c06-8d0e-2afb29df456d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363729} {:answer/id #uuid "69b57ea0-4219-5c9a-98d4-37348b530a78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363730} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d48c24-4cfa-5597-ba88-e96d700bfdf9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "250aeb94-26b3-5db7-a268-7dc6ced96fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363727} :answer-field/id #uuid "84823393-1d61-5298-af3b-2086aa6e6c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11178a4a-447e-5ebe-b505-90599256ee8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37850" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & -4 \\\\ -1 & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of  the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-40{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363732 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54ad46b3-b30c-525a-9ab2-194a63383c61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363734} {:answer/id #uuid "a491ce5a-af53-52f0-9d21-af6e3a8a8177" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363735} {:answer/id #uuid "676169f7-bc8e-5ba4-b577-afc0762481eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363736} {:answer/id #uuid "c68f4464-11fd-5336-a3f8-f66204bf5958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363737} {:answer/id #uuid "ae852346-702f-5d88-ac37-5456474961af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "676169f7-bc8e-5ba4-b577-afc0762481eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363736} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c89b4e1-f82d-55a3-a28f-763563e807ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363733}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c4ebc0e5-899d-5377-baaa-a3d1247291a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83697" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae32878-2848-56d6-96a8-456b6ab2df89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363741} {:answer/id #uuid "17409a4d-957c-5e25-9ab6-0d1832f91786" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363742} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cabb03-6b27-5e7a-98bc-fca2feb600cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363743} {:answer/id #uuid "b14727e0-7de1-59e4-a1e1-e47eccc673b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363744} {:answer/id #uuid "3326c009-069c-5a76-a075-cedcba294f82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1cabb03-6b27-5e7a-98bc-fca2feb600cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363743} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c29b5c-95b0-500a-814b-583bbffa8f9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363740}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e22c72b-c450-5ccd-9f2e-57045a5d397d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83708" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb971587-9dbc-502d-8136-05f596de11cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363748} {:answer/id #uuid "1020ef86-7c42-5eaa-9590-185839669dd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363749} {:answer/id #uuid "caafa56a-b0f9-5681-94d5-e65dea52de2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363750} {:answer/id #uuid "83066b29-3058-5801-968f-15e6c02a41d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363751} {:answer/id #uuid "2038d76c-fff9-5bf5-a668-9a607f0f54c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83066b29-3058-5801-968f-15e6c02a41d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363751} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3e8746-827f-5c31-85e6-e22e3e39d609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c42e4814-ec50-5416-8161-bbb51b0ed62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83710" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363753 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17bd21c4-e230-5005-aa4d-047812372eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363755} {:answer/id #uuid "15466bec-31f6-58ca-8867-3e8cc7ba9695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363756} {:answer/id #uuid "9571d7fa-ba93-5b7a-a6e6-2d7712ab3ee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363757} {:answer/id #uuid "1adfc03e-2ab3-52fa-b45e-65c4c143514f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363758} {:answer/id #uuid "ab02b01b-8a30-5142-a9c7-7e3ab9accd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17bd21c4-e230-5005-aa4d-047812372eab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363755} :answer-field/id #uuid "52437799-2d54-5be8-b6d7-e19713a0836f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "26a7c787-d58c-5868-9f35-380ca2074586" :question/math-academy-id "q-83716" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9ab072-4481-59a2-805b-d89e9d1e6027" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363762} {:answer/id #uuid "10489e03-1a2f-5e7f-be30-f42fc8e7432d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363763} {:answer/id #uuid "beaabb85-6ba9-5104-9d7e-f9466f8bf536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764} {:answer/id #uuid "eefe16c4-3883-58b8-83af-6e0c7e64a242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363765} {:answer/id #uuid "bf26e090-46b7-587a-9585-de999811fc44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beaabb85-6ba9-5104-9d7e-f9466f8bf536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363764} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c76fb4-953c-5fa7-9671-d58c460b6ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Its Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31149a85-3ec5-5052-b872-4fe4ea564c37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=13$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363769} {:answer/id #uuid "f265e444-ca24-5d51-865b-3160c158c937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363770} {:answer/id #uuid "e0f74798-4df0-53a0-a21e-354c2fd0aeb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363771} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5d6085-4ee9-5899-90ab-c3c762df42b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363772} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdab30a-f558-5a60-9a4b-3a2e050665e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0f74798-4df0-53a0-a21e-354c2fd0aeb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363771} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d54674-7042-50b5-8943-de5e4a053921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca9903e3-49ec-5aad-b531-9a0f3deac3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84592" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c2a887a-5180-5578-a732-b60d7c26f19b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363776} {:answer/id #uuid "15eb039c-88ca-52be-817b-9a7800c1f674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363777} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4e50f9-ac5f-56e2-98d7-d5910c559180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363778} {:answer/id #uuid "6cbcc85b-6686-5ce5-b7a3-a499d4898fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363779} {:answer/id #uuid "cc668511-0ccf-5d3d-8a9d-9410056d96f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15eb039c-88ca-52be-817b-9a7800c1f674" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363777} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac58bd82-672d-5030-a429-397627bdd937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363781 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether a Given Matrix Is Diagonalizable Over the Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a62156c-b968-57e8-8974-7c1b4ba6c367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363783} {:answer/id #uuid "101b3006-c077-5e3e-a5b6-02b33d1f8b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363784} {:answer/id #uuid "1c383382-f770-51d7-a2d8-8d674df21526" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363785} {:answer/id #uuid "dc51c4b5-aedb-56e1-85df-8dd8183838f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363786} {:answer/id #uuid "7c95747c-ed23-529f-a91c-e61d6cad7ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c95747c-ed23-529f-a91c-e61d6cad7ad1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363787} :answer-field/id #uuid "ead3919c-5224-5225-8659-554b0d913c97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "457f60a1-a9cc-5ba4-93d9-6cd5c043c941" :question/math-academy-id "q-84599" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -7 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+21) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363788 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d911831-d43b-5217-aa13-6c86953402f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363790} {:answer/id #uuid "616927ef-758e-59b3-9a1d-7e0eda25d3b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363791} {:answer/id #uuid "e43a7b97-5207-55f0-9f7e-1414c06eac3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363792} {:answer/id #uuid "1a71692f-fb6b-5951-9492-a99105e72e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363793} {:answer/id #uuid "1ee9afda-ada5-5ac7-8ac3-58b336211770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a71692f-fb6b-5951-9492-a99105e72e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363793} :answer-field/id #uuid "56727957-d5b4-5b1b-b866-033cd2dec2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363789}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02b892c4-044a-53a5-940f-9a5ebb949669" :question/math-academy-id "q-84607" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "780be5d2-4524-529a-b96d-0010b7dbb2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363797} {:answer/id #uuid "c80fa701-d311-5537-911e-963d3e556ffc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363798} {:answer/id #uuid "e98a8ee1-9825-5ff7-925e-a4128263b80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363799} {:answer/id #uuid "e51f5c63-d013-59ab-8b27-542cb274b937" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363800} {:answer/id #uuid "68748cbd-0a67-5d58-9e80-ef44fe41ffb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "780be5d2-4524-529a-b96d-0010b7dbb2d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363797} :answer-field/id #uuid "460d148a-37ab-5403-9041-893348fa0617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54cd0c24-1bc4-5d0f-9bf4-9dbaab64f62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84610" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-3)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363802 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95" :knowledge-point/title "Diagonalizing a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16db17d4-55cd-57f9-be99-e7f4fa0092f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363804} {:answer/id #uuid "ca34b7df-105e-5308-ae97-d5fc5ee036f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363805} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5fa978-9c85-5bcf-9c7c-4c122b743ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363806} {:answer/id #uuid "678818c9-bcfd-5034-843c-e5ed4c4c509b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363807} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaea40f-757e-51df-8249-71cbdcdd8467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a5fa978-9c85-5bcf-9c7c-4c122b743ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363806} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6b7c6a-be14-571a-8189-e14896588016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "37c618a1-4145-554c-81e2-16575bea3127" :question/math-academy-id "q-84611" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}]}
